      id: "today",
      title: "Today",
      kicker: "Streams, shops and survivors",
      lead: "In about thirty years jazz recording went from a CD reissue boom to a streaming economy that pays fractions of a cent per play, and it now survives on vinyl, Bandcamp, a handful of nimble independents and clubs that barely came through the pandemic.",
      paras: [
        "The compact disc went on sale in Japan in October 1982 and in Europe and North America in March 1983 [1]. For jazz, its first gift was the past. The majors reissued their vaults, and specialists went further. Mosaic Records, founded by Michael Cuscuna and Charlie Lourie in the early 1980s, leased recordings from the majors for three or five years and issued complete box sets, usually in editions of 5,000. The New York Times called it “the most distinctive reissue label in jazz” [2]. By 2000 CDs made up 92.3 percent of the US music market, and sales peaked that year [1].",
        "The decline was fast. In 2018 Americans bought 52 million CDs, less than 6 percent of the 2000 peak [1]. The shops went first. Tower Records filed for bankruptcy for the second time in August 2006, blaming debt, mass discounters and internet piracy among other causes. Its last American stores closed that December [3]. Even ECM, which still calls CDs and vinyl its “preferred mediums,” kept its catalog off streaming services until 14 November 2017. It finally gave in, it said, to counter unauthorized streams and piracy [4].",
        "Streaming does not pay a fixed price per play. Spotify passes about 70 percent of its revenue to rights holders, divided according to each one’s share of total streams. It has reported average per-play payouts ranging from $0.000029 to $0.0084, and in 2013 it said the average was $0.007 [5]. At that rate a million plays earns about $7,000 for all the rights holders together, before any label or publisher pays the musicians. From April 2024 Spotify stopped paying recorded royalties on any track with fewer than 1,000 streams in the previous twelve months. It said such tracks had averaged three cents a month [6]. The musicians’ union UMAW has campaigned for one cent per stream [5].",
        "Bandcamp offered another model. Founded in 2008, it lets artists and labels sell downloads, records and merchandise directly, keeping 15 percent of sales, or 10 percent once an artist has sold more than $5,000 [7]. From 2020, during the pandemic, it waived its share on “Bandcamp Fridays,” which were reported to have raised more than $40 million for artists that year [7]. Bandcamp’s ownership has been unstable. Epic Games bought the company in March 2022. Its staff voted to unionize in March 2023. That autumn Epic sold Bandcamp to Songtradr, and about half of its employees were not offered jobs with the new owner [7].",
        "Vinyl came back. In the first half of 2020 American vinyl sales passed CDs in revenue for the first time since the 1980s, and for the full year vinyl brought in $619.6 million against $483.3 million for CDs [8]. In 2022 vinyl outsold CDs in units for the first time since 1987, and in 2024 Americans bought 43.4 million records worth $954 million [8]. Jazz labels courted collectors. In 2019 Blue Note launched its Tone Poet series of audiophile reissues, chosen and produced by Joe Harley [9]. Since 2024, Japan’s Three Blind Mice has reissued its 1970s catalog from the original analogue tapes [17].",
        "The great jazz names now sit mostly inside one company. Universal Music Group owns Blue Note, which it runs under Capitol Music Group [9]. Verve and Impulse! both belong to Universal’s Verve Label Group [10]. Universal has also distributed the independent ECM worldwide since 1999 [4]. Many of the labels founded by Alfred Lion, Norman Granz and Creed Taylor are now catalog assets on one corporate balance sheet. Younger artists often reach that system after an independent has found them first. Makaya McCraven made his name on International Anthem before recording Deciphering the Message for Blue Note in 2021 [18].",
        "Those independents are where much of today’s jazz is made. Chicago’s International Anthem, launched in 2014, co-releases with Nonesuch and XL [11]. New York’s Pi Recordings issued fewer than a hundred albums in two decades with two people running it [13]. Brainfeeder, a beat producer’s label, released Kamasi Washington’s The Epic in 2015 [16]. Jazz Is Dead, founded by Adrian Younge and Ali Shaheed Muhammad in Los Angeles, records new albums with veterans such as Roy Ayers, Gary Bartz and Azymuth [12]. London has Brownswood. These labels are small and close to the scenes they record, and partnerships with larger firms give them reach.",
        "The rooms were hit hardest. When the COVID-19 pandemic closed live music in March 2020, American independent venues formed the National Independent Venue Association, and about 900 venues joined within a week [14]. Its Save Our Stages Act, introduced in July, was folded into the federal relief bill passed on 21 December 2020 [14]. For some clubs it came too late. New York’s Jazz Standard had opened in 1997 and hosted the Mingus Big Band’s Monday nights, which produced a Grammy-winning live album. It closed permanently on 2 December 2020 after losing its revenue and failing to agree new rent terms [15].",
      ],
      image: { "src": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Kamasi_Washington_Coachella.jpg/960px-Kamasi_Washington_Coachella.jpg", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kamasi_Washington_Coachella.jpg", "caption": "Kamasi Washington, whose Brainfeeder album The Epic carried jazz to festival crowds, performing at Coachella in April 2016.", "credit": "Fred von Lohmann", "license": "CC0" },
